<h2>The suit and the shortcut</h2>
<p>Roshan Lal owned property in Subhadra Colony, Sarai Rohilla, executed a will in 1997 and died in 1998. The beneficiary obtained Letters of Administration with the will annexed in 2018; the grant was never challenged and attained finality. When he sued occupying family members for possession and ₹30,000 monthly damages, the defendants\' answer was that the will gave him only the small shop it described, leaving the rest to flow by inheritance in which they claimed shares. The plaintiff moved under Order XII Rule 6, and the District Judge decreed possession of the property excluding the shop. The defendants\' first appeal under Section 96 CPC put a single substantive question to the High Court: was the scope of the bequest triable?</p>

<h2>How the will was read</h2>
<div class="flow">
<div class="fstep"><strong>Plain words first</strong> — "my above said property" refers to the whole of the premises described in the will, not to the shop alone; the shop reference recorded the property\'s existing state and occupation.</div>
<div class="fstep"><strong>Exclusionary clause</strong> — the bequest "to the exclusion of all other legal heirs" signalled that the testator intended nothing to pass by intestacy to other family members.</div>
<div class="fstep"><strong>Internal contrast</strong> — for another asset, a flat, the testator allotted fixed fractional shares; he knew how to create proportionate bequests and chose not to for this property.</div>
<div class="fstep"><strong>Section 88 inapplicable</strong> — the rule preferring a later irreconcilable clause presupposes two conflicting dispositions; a descriptive reference and an operative bequest do not conflict.</div>
<div class="fstep"><strong>Armchair rule bounded</strong> — surrounding family circumstances assist interpretation but cannot override clear words.</div>
</div>

<h2>Long occupation is not ownership</h2>
<p>The appellants\' fallback — decades of family occupation — fared no better. Permissive possession by family members, however long, generates neither title nor adverse possession against the owner who permitted it. With the Letters of Administration final and unchallenged, the plaintiff\'s representative title to enforce the will was beyond attack in the possession suit. The appeal was dismissed and the part decree affirmed, with the shop tenancy expressly preserved for trial.</p>

<h2>The practice point: pleading against admissions</h2>
<div class="check">
<p>Order XII Rule 6 is the sharpest sword in Delhi\'s civil-suit armoury against dilatory defences, and this judgment shows the anatomy of a successful application: isolate the admissions in the written statement and documents; demonstrate that the residual dispute is purely legal; and carve out genuinely triable fragments — here, the shop tenancy — rather than overreaching. Defendants who wish to avoid a decree on admissions must dispute the foundational facts specifically and plausibly in the written statement itself; a construction argument dressed up as a factual dispute will not buy a trial.</p>
</div>

<div class="note">
<p>Judgments on admissions are appealable like any decree, but the appellate court asks only whether the admissions were clear and the legal question correctly answered. Where both hold, the first appeal fails without the record ever needing oral evidence — exactly what occurred here.</p>
</div>
